+## Revision Model
 
-## Submission and Retrieval
+Every accepted submission becomes one revision of one visit. A revision carries the complete visit content, the submitting worker's identity, the server receipt time, and the base revision the submission was prepared against. The first revision of a visit has no base. Revision content is immutable once stored.
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Each revision is in exactly one state:
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+| State | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `current` | The visit's present record |
+| `open` | A competing revision awaiting coordinator resolution |
+| `superseded` | A former current revision, replaced by a later fast-forward or an adoption |
+| `withdrawn` | A competing revision a coordinator reviewed and chose not to adopt |
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+A visit has exactly one current revision and may have any number of open revisions. A visit has unresolved review work while it has one or more open revisions.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Server-assigned revision identifiers are unique within a visit. Identifiers are opaque; the server receipt time is carried as revision metadata for ordering, not encoded in the identifier. The current-state response exposes the current revision's identifier so tablets can name it as the base of their next edit.
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Submission Envelope
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+| Field | Content |
+|---|---|
+| `visit_id` | Client-generated UUID of the visit |
+| `base_revision` | Identifier of the revision the submission was prepared against; absent on a visit's first submission |
+| `content` | Complete visit payload: catalog plot reference, observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes |
+| `idempotency_key` | Client-generated key scoping retry detection |
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+The submitting worker's identity comes from the authentication context, not the envelope.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+Validation before acceptance covers field types, a plot reference that resolves in the site catalog, and base-revision consistency. A plot reference resolves even when the plot is archived; the catalog retains archived plots for historical lookup. A submission that names a base revision for a visit with no stored revisions is rejected, as is a submission that omits a base revision for a visit that already has revisions, and a submission that names a base revision not stored for that visit. Rejections are reported to the caller and nothing is stored.
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+Any stored revision of the visit — current, open, superseded, or withdrawn — is a valid base. Bases pointing at non-current revisions are the normal offline case: the tablet's base was current when fetched and has since been replaced.
+
+## Acceptance and Divergence
+
+- A submission for a visit UUID with no stored revisions creates the visit's first revision, which becomes current.
+- A valid edit whose base revision is the visit's current revision is accepted as the new current revision; the prior current revision becomes superseded. This fast-forward is the ordinary case — a worker correcting a visit days after it was recorded — and creates no review work.
+- A valid edit whose base revision is a stored revision other than the current revision — whatever that base's own state — is retained as an open competing revision, giving the visit unresolved review work. First arrival wins the fast-forward only provisionally: the divergent submission is never discarded, and a coordinator compares it against the current revision before the visit can return to export.
+
+Divergence does not block capture. While a visit has open revisions, an edit naming the current revision as its base still fast-forwards. Open revisions are not re-anchored when the current revision changes: each remains open and is judged against whatever revision is current when its resolution is issued. A visit leaves the unresolved-work listing only when all of its open revisions have been resolved. Field workers never wait on coordinator throughput.
+
+The acceptance result records the assigned revision identifier and the revision's classification as current or open. An idempotent retry returns this original result rather than re-classifying the submission against the state at retry time. Idempotency keys are scoped to the submitting worker and remembered only for accepted submissions: a retry of a rejected submission is evaluated fresh, and reuse of an accepted key with a different payload is rejected as a client error.
+
+## Coordinator Resolution
+
+A coordinator resolves each open revision by adopting or withdrawing it:
+
+- **Adopt**: the open revision becomes the visit's current revision; the prior current revision becomes superseded.
+- **Withdraw**: the open revision becomes withdrawn; the current revision is unchanged.
+
+A resolution request names the open revision and the current revision it was compared against. If the visit's current revision has changed since the comparison, the resolution is rejected as an error response: no state changes, no resolution record is stored, and the coordinator re-issues the resolution naming the new current revision. The same applies to a resolution naming a revision that is no longer open. This keeps two coordinators and late-arriving tablet syncs from applying a judgment made against a stale comparison.
+
+When a resolution is adopted and other open revisions remain, they stay open and are judged against the newly current revision.
+
+Only applied resolutions are recorded. Each retains the coordinator's identity, the contributing submission IDs — the open revision and the current revision it was judged against — and the outcome. Resolution selects among stored revisions; it never modifies stored content. Content changes enter the store only as new submissions through the envelope above, from any authenticated author including coordinators.
+
+## Retrieval
+
+**Current state.** Retrieving a visit returns its current revision identifier and content, whether unresolved review work exists, and the identifiers of any open competing revisions.
+
+**Submission history.** Retrieving a visit's history returns every stored submission with its author, server receipt time, base revision, and revision state, plus any resolution records.
+
+**Unresolved-work listing.** Listing unresolved visits returns each visit with unresolved review work together with its current revision identifier and open competing revision identifiers, so review tooling can enumerate its queue. Batch and pagination shape are sized with review-export's queue screen.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency policy for independently edited visits | Whole-visit revisions: full-content submissions named against a base revision; a coordinator resolves each divergence | Field-aware three-way merge: tablets send structured changes against a base snapshot; disjoint field edits combine automatically, overlaps go to review | Automatic merge rules could combine scientifically related edits — a species re-identification and its cover estimate — without a human noticing, and the server cannot infer those relationships from field names. The team accepts a larger review queue over maintaining merge rules with that risk. Sequential corrections never create review work, so the queue grows only on genuine divergence. Full analysis and revisit triggers: decisions/whole-visit-revisions.md |
+| Effect of divergence on the current record | The first-arriving valid edit fast-forwards; later divergent edits are retained as open revisions for coordinator review | Hold all same-base edits for review regardless of arrival order; first-arrival-wins silently, as the spreadsheet imports being replaced did | The server cannot know a second edit is in flight when the first arrives, so first arrival sets only a provisional current record. The constraint that arrival order is not evidence of authority is honored by mandatory review of every divergent arrival, not by blocking the ordinary case. |
+| Capture during unresolved review | Edits naming the current revision continue to fast-forward while open revisions exist | Freeze a visit's edits until its open revisions are resolved | Field workers must not wait on coordinator throughput; open revisions are re-judged against the newer current revision at resolution time. |
+| Resolution conflict handling | A resolution names the current revision it was compared against and is rejected if that revision is no longer current | Apply resolutions unconditionally | Two coordinators plus tablets syncing days late make stale comparisons likely; rejecting a stale resolution forces re-review against the live current record and prevents an adjudication made against superseded content. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether a divergent submission whose content is identical to the current revision (two workers making the same correction independently) auto-resolves as a no-op or always goes to review — decide with review-export's queue sizing.
+2. Whether a withdrawn revision can be re-opened for review, or whether a worker wanting it reconsidered must resubmit through the envelope.
+3. Batch and pagination shape of the unresolved-work listing — sized against review-export's queue screen.
